Fillets: the corner where a rounding ends against a face (OnSame), when the
opposite face is split along an edge at the corner's vertex into two faces
of one surface (mitcad#239). Example: a slot cut into the side of a
cylinder whose side is two faces split at the slot's back wall; the slot's
top edges rounded. The rounding's contact on the side face ends on that
split edge, which lies in the face at the end. The missing end of the
prolonged edge (the edge between the face at the end and the face beyond
the split) was put on the opposite face, which does not hold that edge,
and the split edge was left whole: the topological build kept the split
edge's pieces under the rounding and gave an unorientable shell.

- PerformOneCorner, OnSame: where the opposite contact ends on an edge of
  the opposite face at the corner's vertex (before it is moved onto the
  face at the end), the opposite face does not hold the prolonged edge,
  and the other face of the prolonged edge holds that edge too, the
  missing end is computed on that other face (its p-curve there, the
  prolonged edge's orientation in it), and the edge is cut where the
  contact ends: its part from the vertex, under the rounding, is dropped.
  Elsewhere nothing changes.
